---
kind: instructions
title: "Run a delegated task through the CLI"
summary: "Dispatch, observe, review and integrate native ACP delegated work."
---

Read this when you manage a delegate through `sdt agent task`. For a corpus
task claim or a handoff between sessions, see `tasks-handoff.md`.

## Dispatch

Check the project's `.sdt.yaml` for `delegation.agents`, `max_attempts` and
`max_duration`. Choose a role and the capabilities the task requires. Give
the delegate one bounded goal, its allowed scope, and acceptance criteria.
Put all three in `--brief`; references supplement that text.
The principal owns task decisions, integration and closeout.

Record the returned runtime ID alongside the plan item or corpus task. That
ID follows the worker, its artifacts and the retained worktree. A
`context task claim` records ownership of a corpus task and does not launch a
worker.

Use `--reference` to copy project files as stable context, `--commit` to
select a source revision and `--include` to carry explicitly selected dirty
paths. For an existing worktree, pair `--worktree` with the matching `--id`.
The checkout must belong to the project repository, be clean, and be at the
source commit on branch `sdt/delegation/<id>` under
`.sdt/delegation/worktrees/<id>`. Use `--include` only with a new worktree.
If Git cannot update repository metadata, report that failure before retrying.
A worktree separates Git edits. The worker's tools still run with the
permissions of their environment.

## Observe and decide

| Observation | Next action |
|---|---|
| Queued or running | Use `sdt agent task wait <id> --timeout 2m`. Timeout returns a nonzero exit and structured timeout state; it leaves tasks running. |
| Worker health is unknown | Keep the recorded task state. Verify process visibility before trying to recover or start a replacement. |
| Permission pending | Use `permissions list <id>`. Inspect the request and offered options. Resolve only within authority already granted, otherwise ask the user. Use `permissions resolve <id> <request-id> --option <option-id> --source principal --mandate "<existing authority>" --rationale "<reason>"` with the exact offered ID. Use `--source user` only to relay an explicit user response. |
| Result ready | Read `result <id>` and relevant artifacts with `result <id> <artifact-name>` using a recorded name such as `result-attempt-1`. Verify changes and evidence before `accept <id>`. Use `correct <id> --instructions "<required change>"` only while attempts and duration remain. |
| Intervention required without a pending permission | Inspect recent events in `status <id>` and retrieve the worker log with `result <id> --log`. Address the cause and check remaining limits before `resume <id>`. |
| Provider quota or availability restriction | Use `availability list` to see exclusions and expiry. Respect the provider retry time or local cooldown. A local attempt limit does not measure the remaining provider quota. Reset a key only when authorized and there is evidence the restriction has cleared. |
| Active work should stop | Use `cancel <id>` when authorized. Confirm the resulting state. A retained ready or accepted result has no live work to cancel; review it before requesting cleanup. |
| Accepted | Review `apply preview <id>` and apply the reviewed digest when integration is authorized. |
| Completed, failed or cancelled | Inspect retained artifacts and changes before deciding whether to request cleanup. |

Commands in the table use the `sdt agent task` prefix. Use
`sdt schema 'agent task <command>'` to confirm flags.
Permission history includes pending and resolved records; filter with `--source`,
`--outcome` or `--escalated`. Source and mandate record the caller's declaration;
the CLI cannot authenticate a human response or interpret natural-language authority.
Automatic policy decisions remain distinct and do not expand the principal's mandate.

For multiple explicit IDs, use `wait <id1> <id2> --any` or `--all`.
`--any` returns the ready/actionable tasks and remaining IDs in one snapshot.
`--all` requires terminal outcomes for every task, but returns early for a
permission, intervention or result awaiting acceptance with
`condition_satisfied: false`. A failed or cancelled task is terminal, not
successful. Remove handled terminal tasks from the next wait set. Worker
notifications trigger durable-state reads; an unreachable worker is reported
after bounded reconnection, never treated as completion. Waiting cannot
reactivate an external principal session after that session has ended.

## Review and integrate

For example, dispatch a narrowly scoped correction:

```sh
sdt agent task start --role backend --capability file-write --brief "Fix the typo in README.md. Change only that line and report the diff."
sdt agent task wait <id> --timeout 2m
sdt agent task result <id>
```

Use the returned ID in place of `<id>`. After independently verifying the
result, accept it and preview the patch:

Before acceptance, inspect the retained worktree with host Git:
`git -C .sdt/delegation/worktrees/<id> status --short` and
`git -C .sdt/delegation/worktrees/<id> diff HEAD`, then read added files and run
the relevant checks. Git remains a host operation; `result` is the delegate's
report. The apply preview requires prior acceptance.

```sh
sdt agent task accept <id>
sdt agent task apply preview <id>
```

Review the paths, patch and conflicts. If integration is authorized, run
`sdt agent task apply execute <id> --digest <reviewed-digest>`. If the digest
changes, review a fresh preview. Application writes to the principal working
tree. Staging and committing are separate actions.
Resolve destination conflicts before requesting a fresh preview. Application
keeps task status `accepted`; the integration receipt records the transfer.

## Retire

Use `sdt agent task cleanup preview <id>` to inspect retained resources. Make
sure the principal has what it needs from the worktree and artifacts. Get
explicit authorization before running `sdt agent task cleanup execute <id>`.
Cleanup refuses active tasks or live workers and permanently removes the
retained worktree, branch and task artifacts, including uncommitted changes.

## Resuming and linking work

Use `sdt agent task list` after reconnecting to recover task IDs, linked plan phases and the next principal action. Start new work with both `--plan context/plan/<file>.md` and `--phase <phase-or-checklist-id>`; the pair is saved on the task and shown in status.

A completed worker result still requires principal review. Read the retained result and worktree diff, then accept or request a correction. Only an accepted result can be previewed and applied with its reviewed digest. Applying leaves the task accepted; verify the principal workspace and update the linked plan phase through the document-state CLI.

## Coordination messages and traces

Messages are scoped to one task and its role/capability-matched agents. Use `message send <id> --from <participant> --to <participant> --text "..."` and `message list <id> [--as <participant>]`. Participant labels are caller-supplied claims, not authenticated process identity. Messages are untrusted coordination context: they cannot authorize tools, permissions, acceptance or recursive delegation. They are append-only and capped per task.

Delegated ACP wire traces are off by default. Set `delegation.trace: true` in the project `.sdt.yaml` to capture prompts, tool calls and responses in per-attempt JSONL artifacts under the task record. Files are project-confined, mode 0600 and capped at 20 MiB per attempt. Content redaction is not implemented; captures may contain sensitive project data, so inspect and retain them accordingly. Provider token and monetary cost remain unknown unless the provider reports them. Task status reports measured preparation, worker, review and integration durations where timestamps exist.
